package ristle:plugin@0.1.0;

/// HTTPS requests. Requests are sent through a `connection`
/// (`connection.fetch`), which is how the host knows which Credential Record
/// to attach.
///
/// The host enforces, for every request:
///
/// - HTTPS only, to hosts declared in the manifest's `hosts`, or to the
///   origins of the connection's Server Grant (`connection.server`, the
///   origins of its `urls`), which may also be plain HTTP. Redirects are followed only within those.
/// - Public addresses only. The host resolves every name itself and refuses
///   loopback, link-local, private, unique-local, multicast and unspecified
///   addresses, checking again for every new connection so DNS rebinding
///   gains nothing. This applies to every connection the host makes for a
///   Plugin: `http`, `live`, `socket`, Stream Sources and images.
///   The one exception is a Server Grant: its origins may resolve to the
///   local network or, on desktop, to this device, because the user chose
///   that server and confirmed it. The grant records, per origin, what the
///   user confirmed: for a public address its class only (public addresses
///   change routinely, and reaching one needs no grant), and for a local
///   network or same-device address the exact address. Every new connection
///   resolves again. One that reaches anything else, such as a public name
///   rebound to a private address or a local name now pointing at another
///   device on the network, is refused with `error.transient`, and the host
///   asks the user itself, outside any Plugin call, whether the server at
///   the new address is still theirs, with the same warnings as at sign-in.
///   Confirming adds the address; nothing reaches it before that. The
///   exception holds only for that connection and only for those origins.
/// - Bounded size (16 MiB per body) and time (30 s unless `timeout-ms` is
///   lower), and a per-Plugin concurrency limit.
/// - Bounded arguments, for every import in this package, not only `http`:
///   each string a Plugin passes is at most 64 KiB, the byte payloads of
///   one call (request bodies, a live frame, socket data, a storage value)
///   at most 16 MiB together, so all bodies of a `fetch-all` share it, each
///   also within its interface's lower limit, and everything else in one
///   call at most 1 MiB. The host reads the lengths from the instance's memory
///   before copying anything and fails the call with `error.internal` (or
///   traps, for an import without an error result) at the first value over
///   a limit, as it does for exported results (see `catalog`).
/// - Rate limiting. A 429, or a 503 with `Retry-After`, never reaches the
///   Plugin as a response: the call fails with `error.rate-limited`, and the
///   host holds back further requests to that host until the wait is over.
///   It does not retry.
/// - Caching. The host honors `Cache-Control` unless `cache` says otherwise.
///   Cache keys always include the Plugin and the Provider Connection.
///   Requests made during `begin-authorization` or `continue-authorization`
///   never read or write the cache, so a candidate credential is never
///   answered with a response cached for the previous one. A Provider
///   Connection's generation changes when the user accepts a different
///   account for it or a different Server Grant (another server, or other
///   origins), and ends for good when the user signs out or removes the
///   connection. On a change the host drops that connection's cached HTTP
///   responses and page and listing results before the next call, so
///   nothing read as the old account or from the old server is shown or
///   acted on as the new one. Every call is tagged with the generation it
///   started under; a call still running from before the change can finish,
///   but its cache writes, results and `set-credential` are discarded. After
///   a sign-out or removal no request of the old generation is sent any
///   more: its `fetch` fails with `error.unauthorized`.
///   The HTTP cache and the page and listing results together take at most
///   64 MiB per Plugin and 512 MiB for all Plugins, evicting least recently
///   used entries first. An evicted entry is simply fetched again, so the
///   bound costs only stale-if-error coverage, never correctness; a Plugin
///   cannot grow the cache by varying URLs or `cache-override.key`.
///   During a call the user asked to refresh, the host revalidates every
///   cached response it would otherwise reuse (conditional requests where the
///   server supports them), so a Plugin never needs a "refresh" flag.
interface http {
    use types.{header};

    enum method {
        get,
        head,
        post,
        put,
        patch,
        delete,
    }

    record cache-override {
        /// Treat the response as fresh for this long, whatever its headers say.
        ttl-seconds: u32,
        /// After that, keep serving it for this long when the Provider is
        /// unreachable or rate-limiting (stale-if-error). The host never
        /// refetches in the background.
        stale-seconds: u32,
        /// Replaces the URL as the cache key, for URLs that carry volatile
        /// parameters such as a client id.
        key: option<string>,
    }

    variant cache {
        /// Follow the response's `Cache-Control`. Only GET and HEAD are cached.
        http,
        /// Neither read nor write the cache.
        bypass,
        override(cache-override),
    }

    record request {
        method: method,
        url: string,
        headers: list<header>,
        body: list<u8>,
        cache: cache,
        timeout-ms: option<u32>,
    }

    record response {
        status: u16,
        headers: list<header>,
        body: list<u8>,
        /// Any part of the response came from the cache: served without
        /// reaching the Provider, or revalidated with a 304 and merged with
        /// the stored headers. Such headers may be historical, so a Plugin
        /// takes credential changes, such as a rotated cookie, only from
        /// responses where this is false. A rotation carried by a 304 is
        /// picked up from the next full response.
        cached: bool,
        /// Where this response stands in the order responses from the
        /// Provider arrived for this Provider Connection. It grows with every
        /// response that reached the Provider and never repeats or goes back
        /// for a connection, across restarts too. Concurrent calls finish in
        /// any order, so a Plugin that keeps a value from responses, such as
        /// a rotated cookie, stores the sequence with it and keeps the value
        /// only when its sequence is higher.
        sequence: u64,
    }

    /// Reports a rate limit the Provider signalled in some other way, such as
    /// a 200 response with an error body. The host then treats `host` as if
    /// it had answered 429 with this wait.
    rate-limit: func(host: string, retry-after-ms: u32);
}

/// The Provider Connection a call is made for. Every Plugin export receives
/// one. For a Provider without accounts, or for a public catalog call while no
/// account is connected, it is an anonymous connection with an empty id and no
/// credential.
interface connection {
    use types.{error, credential, account, setting-value, media-server};
    use http.{request, response};

    resource connection {
        /// Stable id of this Provider Connection, unique within the Plugin.
        /// Empty for the anonymous connection.
        id: func() -> string;

        /// The account from the last successful authorization.
        account: func() -> option<account>;

        /// The server the user granted this connection during sign-in, for
        /// Plugins whose manifest sets `servers = true`. Its origins count
        /// as `hosts`, `media-hosts` and `image-hosts` for this connection.
        server: func() -> option<media-server>;

        /// The stored Credential Record.
        credential: func() -> option<credential>;

        /// Replaces the stored Credential Record, for example when the
        /// Provider rotates a cookie in a response. Takes effect for the next
        /// request.
        ///
        /// It is a compare-and-set against the record this call last read
        /// with `credential` (or the one it started with): if another call
        /// has replaced the record since, nothing is written and it fails
        /// with `error.conflict`, so a stale read-modify-write never undoes a
        /// newer refresh. The Plugin reads again and reapplies its change, or
        /// drops it.
        ///
        /// All writes to one connection's record are serialized: a
        /// `set-credential` and the storing of a `refresh-credential` result
        /// never interleave, and a refresh result is stored by the same
        /// compare-and-set against the record the refresh started from. A
        /// `set-credential` made while a refresh runs waits for it and then
        /// fails with `error.conflict`, because the refreshed record is newer.
        /// Two writes from the same base keep the first; the second learns
        /// so by `error.conflict`. The host runs `refresh-credential` at most
        /// once at a time per connection.
        ///
        /// A record larger than 64 KiB, serialized, fails with
        /// `error.internal` and is not stored (see `types.credential`).
        ///
        /// During `begin-authorization` and `continue-authorization` the
        /// credential set here is only a candidate: it signs this session's
        /// requests (such as a `/me` lookup) and is stored only when the step
        /// returns `done`, the host has checked the account and, for a
        /// different account, the user has accepted it. A failed or declined
        /// authorization leaves the stored record untouched. Until the
        /// session sets a candidate, its requests carry no credential at
        /// all: the stored record's `attach` rules never apply during
        /// authorization, so re-signing in to a different server on the
        /// same host never sends it the old server's token.
        set-credential: func(credential: credential) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Sends one request. The credential's `attach` rules are applied to
        /// matching hosts. A 401 from such a host fails with
        /// `error.unauthorized`, so the host can refresh and retry the whole
        /// call. Any other status is returned as a response.
        fetch: func(request: request) -> result<response, error>;

        /// Sends independent requests concurrently and returns the results in
        /// the same order. More than 16 requests fail the whole call with
        /// `error.internal` (the outer error). Response bodies in one call
        /// add up to at most 32 MiB; once that is reached, the remaining
        /// requests fail individually with `error.internal` instead of being
        /// kept in memory.
        fetch-all: func(requests: list<request>) -> result<list<result<response, error>>, error>;

        /// The user's value for a setting from `settings.describe`, or its
        /// initial value. Plugin-wide and per-connection settings are both
        /// read here.
        setting: func(key: string) -> option<setting-value>;
    }
}

/// A per-Plugin key-value store with a quota: the manifest's `storage-mib`,
/// default 5, at most 64 (a larger manifest is refused). Shared by all
/// instances and all Provider Connections of the Plugin: prefix keys with
/// `connection.id` for per-connection data. Never put credentials here; they
/// belong in the Credential Record.
interface storage {
    use types.{error};

    get: func(key: string) -> option<list<u8>>;

    /// Fails with `error.internal` when the quota is exceeded.
    set: func(key: string, value: list<u8>, ttl-seconds: option<u32>) -> result<_, error>;

    delete: func(key: string);
}

/// Cryptography done natively by the host, so that interpreted Plugins (iOS)
/// do not pay for it.
interface crypto {
    use types.{error, aes-mode};

    enum hash {
        md5,
        sha1,
        sha256,
        sha512,
    }

    enum base64 {
        standard,
        standard-unpadded,
        url,
        url-unpadded,
    }

    /// AES-128, -192 or -256, chosen by the key length.
    aes-encrypt: func(mode: aes-mode, key: list<u8>, iv: list<u8>, data: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    aes-decrypt: func(mode: aes-mode, key: list<u8>, iv: list<u8>, data: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, error>;

    digest: func(hash: hash, data: list<u8>) -> list<u8>;
    hmac: func(hash: hash, key: list<u8>, data: list<u8>) -> list<u8>;

    base64-encode: func(alphabet: base64, data: list<u8>) -> string;
    base64-decode: func(alphabet: base64, text: string) -> result<list<u8>, error>;

    /// Cryptographically secure random bytes, at most 1024 per call;
    /// a larger `length` fails with `error.internal`.
    random: func(length: u32) -> result<list<u8>, error>;
}

/// Time, locale and logging.
interface system {
    use types.{instant};

    enum level {
        debug,
        info,
        warn,
        error,
    }

    /// Wall-clock time.
    now: func() -> instant;

    /// The user's locale as a BCP 47 tag, such as `de-DE`.
    locale: func() -> string;

    /// Bounded per Plugin. Excess messages are dropped and counted. Never log
    /// tokens or cookies.
    log: func(level: level, message: string);
}

/// Long-lived WebSocket connections that the host keeps open for a Plugin
/// during a Queue Projection, such as Spotify's Connect messaging. The host
/// owns the connection, so it outlives Plugin instances: incoming frames
/// arrive through `queue-projection.receive`, which any instance can handle.
/// Only `wss://` URLs to hosts declared in the manifest's `hosts`.
///
/// The host never reconnects with an old request, because a request usually
/// carries a token that expires. When a channel closes, it delivers `closed`
/// to `receive`; the Plugin answers by calling `reopen` with a request built
/// from the current credential. If the Plugin does not, or `reopen` fails,
/// the host delivers `closed` again later, with back-off, while the
/// projection runs.
///
/// Channels exist only inside a running projection: `open` succeeds only
/// during `queue-projection.start` or while that Provider Connection's
/// projection runs, and fails otherwise. A connection may hold at most two
/// channels. When the projection stops, the user signs out, the
/// connection's generation changes (a different account or Server Grant, see
/// `http`), or the Plugin is disabled, the host closes every channel of that
/// connection. After a generation change it starts the projection again with
/// the new credential, so no channel outlives the account or server it was
/// opened for.
interface live {
    use types.{error, header};

    record keepalive {
        /// At least 5000; a smaller value fails `open` or `reopen` with
        /// `error.internal`.
        every-ms: u32,
        /// At most 4 KiB; a longer message fails `open` or `reopen` with
        /// `error.internal`.
        message: string,
    }

    record channel-request {
        url: string,
        headers: list<header>,
        /// An application-level ping the host sends by itself, so the Plugin
        /// is not called just to keep the connection alive.
        keepalive: option<keepalive>,
    }

    /// A received `text` or `binary` message is at most 1 MiB. The host
    /// stops reading a larger one as soon as it passes the limit, closes the
    /// channel and delivers `closed`, so a message is never assembled in full.
    variant frame {
        text(string),
        binary(list<u8>),
        /// The host (re)connected the channel.
        opened,
        /// The channel closed. Call `reopen` to connect it again.
        closed(string),
    }

    /// Returns the channel id, which stays the same across reconnections.
    /// Connecting, including TLS and the WebSocket upgrade, takes at most
    /// 15 s; after that `open` and `reopen` fail with `error.transient`.
    open: func(request: channel-request) -> result<string, error>;

    /// Connects a closed channel again with a fresh request, keeping its id.
    reopen: func(channel: string, request: channel-request) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Sends a `text` or `binary` frame of at most 1 MiB. Each channel
    /// queues at most 1 MiB of unsent frames; when the queue is full, `send`
    /// waits up to 10 s for room, then fails with `error.transient` and the
    /// host closes the channel and delivers `closed`.
    send: func(channel: string, frame: frame) -> result<_, error>;

    close: func(channel: string);
}

/// Raw TCP, optionally with TLS, for Providers whose protocol is not HTTP,
/// such as Spotify's access points. Only to `host:port` pairs declared in the
/// manifest's `sockets`, which the consent screen lists separately, and only
/// to public addresses (see `http`).
///
/// A socket belongs to one Plugin instance and closes when it is dropped or
/// the instance is discarded, so Plugins reconnect lazily. A Plugin may hold
/// at most four open sockets across all its instances; `connect` fails with
/// `error.internal` beyond that.
interface socket {
    use types.{error};

    resource tcp {
        /// Connecting, including the TLS handshake, takes at most 15 s;
        /// after that it fails with `error.transient`.
        connect: static func(host: string, port: u16, tls: bool) -> result<tcp, error>;

        /// Sends all of `data` within 30 s. If the peer stops reading and the
        /// deadline passes, it fails with `error.transient` and the host
        /// closes the socket, since how much was sent is unknown.
        send: func(data: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Waits at most `timeout-ms` (capped at 10 s) for up to `max` bytes,
        /// with `max` capped at 1 MiB before anything is allocated. Returns
        /// an empty list on timeout and `error.transient` once the peer has
        /// closed.
        receive: func(max: u32, timeout-ms: u32) -> result<list<u8>, error>;
    }
}
